# Commands and results

- `./gradlew.bat check -x :cap4k-plugin-pipeline-gradle:test --no-daemon`: PASS. Gradle completed 217 tasks; 24 executed and 193 up-to-date.
- `./gradlew.bat :cap4k-plugin-pipeline-gradle:test --no-daemon`: PASS. 236 tests were discovered; 235 passed, one environment-conditional PostgreSQL test was skipped, and none failed. Runtime was 17 minutes 42 seconds.
- Focused managed-policy resolver, JPA UoW, soft-delete, and pipeline functional regressions: PASS. This includes all 16 `StrongIdUowRuntimeTest` cases and the loaded-other-aggregate collection-mutation guard.
- `python scripts/validate-cap4k-generator-inputs.py`: PASS with `OK: no issues found.`
- `git diff --check`: PASS; Git emitted only line-ending normalization warnings.
- Active legacy annotation and DSL search: PASS. Remaining `@IdStrategy` and `@Inherited` strings occur only in rejection tests; no active legacy extension entry remains.
- Supplemental only-engine migration verification: direct Kotlin main/test compilation, ServiceLoader smoke, and JUnit Platform execution all passed; 23 of 23 tests passed.

# Skipped checks

- The real PostgreSQL integration case was skipped because a PostgreSQL service and its environment configuration were not available. H2 runtime coverage plus canonical planning and generated-code compilation cover the supported behavior, but this report does not claim a real PostgreSQL read/write run.
- The only-engine standard Gradle wrapper is not counted as passed. Earlier attempts encountered external Maven TLS failure and a later Kotlin compilation hang; direct compilation, ServiceLoader smoke, and direct JUnit execution provide the supplemental migration evidence instead.

# Spec consistency

The implementation matches the confirmed Comet contract and complete implementation scope. It retains the fixed pipeline, provides one root Pipeline Extension SPI with declarative contributions, resolves exact managed-field policy keys into the canonical model, generates runtime catalog metadata, assigns application IDs no later than aggregate admission, and stabilizes JPA changes as candidate detection, enrichment, final dirty detection, then flush.

The final design spec records the two review corrections: creation input is legal only when INSERT authority is caller, framework, or managed-handler owned; and each enricher is guarded against mutations to every managed persistence-context entity, including collection changes on entities loaded during enrichment.

No public multi-persistence-provider SPI, read-model contract, or tenant-wide query-isolation promise was introduced. Checked-in Factory and Behavior generation continues to use authoritative `SKIP` behavior.

# Known limitations and risks

- The supported persistence implementation remains JPA/Hibernate. Pipeline Extension is available now, while a public multi-provider persistence SPI remains deferred until a second provider creates concrete requirements.
- Database-generated policies are covered by planning, rendering, compilation, and provider-oriented lifecycle tests, but not yet by one real database insert/update readback test.
- Semantic time adapters are covered in resolver and registry layers. There is no single end-to-end `Instant` to `LocalDateTime` catalog-to-Spring-to-UoW-to-flush test yet.
- There is no dedicated Spring startup test for every invalid admission-only custom-accessor footprint; resolver and registry validation cover the underlying constraints.

# Conclusion

PASS. All 15 acceptance criteria have concrete evidence, the complete implementation scope is present, the cap4k repository checks and full pipeline plugin suite pass, and the remaining limitations are explicit, bounded, and do not contradict the confirmed contract.
